T1  - Implementation Strategy Intensity and Fidelity in School-Based Health
Promotion: Results From a Cluster Randomized Effectiveness Trial
JF  - International Trials Register Quarterly
PY  - 2021
U3  - Journal Article
Clinical Trial
AB  - Advancing widespread adoption of obesity prevention programs in schools
requires understanding which implementation support strategies most effectively
promote sustained program delivery. We randomized 45 school clusters to receive
either standard implementation support or enhanced implementation support
combining multiple reinforcement and linkage strategies over two academic
years. Fidelity assessment revealed that approximately 75% of program
components were delivered to the intended standards across both implementation
arms. The enhanced support bundle did not produce significantly greater
component delivery adherence compared to standard support. Two specific
enhanced strategies (provider educational sessions and intersectoral
coordination) were adopted by only a minority of participating schools due to
reported staffing constraints and competing priorities. In contrast, parental
participation and receipt of core health messages were more strongly influenced
by school demographics and family characteristics than by implementation
strategy intensity. These findings highlight that implementation success is
complex and contextual, requiring adaptive refinement rather than static
strategy application.
LA  - eng
DO  - 10.5555/medidex.synthetic.0061
N1  - Underlying cluster randomized hybrid effectiveness-implementation trial
comparing standard school-based health promotion with Healthy School Start
program with implementation strategy optimization.
