T1  - Costimulatory Blockade After Initial Biologic Response in Psoriasis: Relapse
Prevention and Long-Term Disease Control
JF  - Digital Health Trials Journal
PY  - 2019
U3  - Journal Article
Clinical Trial
AB  - Patients with psoriasis who respond favorably to biologic therapy face
uncertainty regarding optimal long-term disease management strategies. We
evaluated whether switching to an alternative costimulatory-blocking biologic
agent could prevent disease relapse following withdrawal of an initial biologic
therapy. One hundred eight adults with moderate-to-severe psoriasis received
lead-in therapy with an established biologic agent, and 91 responders were
subsequently randomized to either continue the original biologic or transition
to costimulatory blockade, with both withdrawn at week 39. Over the subsequent
observation period through week 88, approximately 91% of participants assigned
to the alternative costimulatory-blocking agent experienced psoriasis relapse
compared to 87% in the continued-therapy group—a difference that was not
statistically significant. Median time from treatment discontinuation to
disease relapse was similar in both groups (approximately 5 weeks). Molecular
assessment of lesional tissue revealed that costimulatory blockade did not
adequately suppress the pathogenic inflammatory cascade responsible for
psoriasis. Safety profiles remained acceptable in both treatment arms. These
findings indicate that costimulatory blockade alone is insufficient to maintain
control after withdrawal of the original biologic therapy.
LA  - eng
DO  - 10.5555/medidex.synthetic.0063
N1  - Underlying double-blind randomized controlled trial comparing continued therapy
with established biologic agent with costimulatory blockade with abatacept
after prior biologic therapy.
