AU  - Schmidt, Aisling
T1  - Postoperative Pain Outcomes Following Root Canal Treatment: Electronic Apex
Locators versus Radiographic Working Length Determination
JF  - Journal of Translational Patient Care
PY  - 2021
U3  - Journal Article
Clinical Trial
AB  - One hundred twenty-five patients with asymptomatic irreversible pulpitis
underwent single-visit root canal treatment with working length determined via
one of five methods. Postoperative pain assessments revealed that discomfort
gradually diminished over the 48-hour evaluation window in all treatment
groups. Statistical analysis comparing pain intensities across the five working
length determination approaches showed no clinically or statistically
significant differences at any assessment timepoint. Pain patterns were similar
whether patients underwent electronic locator-guided or radiography-based
working length determination. Variables including age, sex, tooth location, and
analgesic intake patterns did not demonstrate significant associations with
postoperative pain across groups. Medication use for post-treatment discomfort
management showed no meaningful variation among the five treatment approaches.
These findings indicate that under standardised clinical conditions, the
specific working length determination methodology does not substantially
influence pain experience following root canal completion in this patient
population with straightforward single-rooted anatomy.
LA  - eng
DO  - 10.5555/medidex.synthetic.0127
N1  - Underlying randomised trial, five treatment arms comparing periapical
radiography for working length determination with various electronic apex
locators for working length determination.
