AU  - Csik, Omar
T1  - Long-term tolerability and patient-reported outcomes with cosmetic versus
prescription acne therapy
JF  - Journal of Primary Care Innovation
PY  - 2021
U3  - Journal Article
Clinical Trial
AB  - Secondary analysis examined tolerability and quality-of-life endpoints from the
12-week acne treatment comparison. Skin irritation symptoms were mild to absent
in the cosmetic regimen group across all assessment windows, whereas
prescription therapy produced higher irritation scores peaking at week 4.
Facial skin hydration, assessed via transepidermal water loss measurements,
showed greater preservation with cosmetic treatment. Post-inflammatory
hyperpigmentation improved more favorably in those with baseline darker skin
types receiving cosmetic therapy. Patient-reported confidence in appearance and
social comfort improved significantly more in the cosmetic group. Among
patients completing the full 12-week course, 96.8% in the cosmetic arm versus
82.6% in the prescription arm reported willingness to continue their assigned
regimen. Subset analysis in darker skin types (Fitzpatrick IV-VI) revealed
superior tolerability with cosmetic therapy while maintaining comparable
efficacy. These findings suggest the cosmetic regimen may offer particular
advantage in diverse populations with sensitivity concerns.
LA  - eng
