T1  - Expanded TB screening does not improve diagnostic yield or mortality in
hospitalized HIV-positive patients
JF  - Journal of Nursing-Led Care Research
PY  - 2016
U3  - Journal Article
Clinical Trial
AB  - Background: Early TB diagnosis may improve outcomes in hospitalized HIV
patients. We evaluated whether expanded diagnostic testing from multiple
specimen types improves case detection and treatment initiation. Setting: 11
hospitals (Tanzania and Mozambique), Sept 2022-March 2024. Participants: 1172
hospitalized adults with HIV (582 expanded arm, 590 standard arm); 61% female,
median CD4 232 cells/μL. Intervention: Expanded testing (sputum/stool/urine
plus urine antigen) versus standard symptom-based testing. Results:
Microbiologically confirmed TB was detected in 16.0% of expanded arm and 15.3%
of standard arm, with no significant difference (0.7%, 95% CI -3.4 to 4.8,
p=0.73). Treatment initiation within 72 hours occurred in 93 expanded versus 90
standard arm participants (p>0.05). 8-week mortality was 25.8% (expanded)
versus 28.8% (standard) (HR 0.86, 95% CI 0.69-1.07, p=0.18). Time from
admission to TB treatment did not differ significantly between arms (median
0.98 vs 0.92 days, p=0.72). In the control arm, 85.6% had TB-compatible
symptoms yet only 60.6% provided sputum samples. Conclusions: Expanded
diagnostic strategies did not improve TB case detection, treatment timeliness,
or 8-week survival despite increased testing.
LA  - eng
